mean predicted 0.0735 vs actual positive rate 0.0449
expected positives 8,950 vs actual 5,475  ->  1.63x overcount
ROC AUC and PR AUC cannot see this: both are rank-based, so the ordering
can be good while the magnitude is wrong by this factor. It matters the
moment a score is read as a probability - counting modified sites per
cell line in Task 2 does exactly that.

Thresholds
----------
PR AUC and ROC AUC integrate over every threshold, which is what makes
them right for comparing two models and useless for using one. Counting
modified sites needs an operating point; these are three, and none of
them is a recommendation.
                 threshold  precision  recall     f1  predicted
operating point                                                
f1_max              0.5250     0.4970  0.5677 0.5300       6254
count_matched       0.5950     0.5253  0.5262 0.5258       5484
half                0.5000     0.4856  0.5792 0.5283       6530

5,475 sites really are positive, out of 121,838.
  f1_max         balances the two errors, which is a default, not a finding
  count_matched  calls as many sites as are really positive - the point a
                 *count* wants, and it says nothing about picking the
                 right ones
  half           0.5, the threshold everyone reaches for without looking

A site count swings 2.2x between threshold 0.3 (9,553 sites) and 0.7 (4,375 sites).
Both are thresholds someone could choose with a straight face, so that
ratio is the span of an unforced choice rather than a statistical
interval. Any Task 2 claim of the form 'cell line X has N modified sites'
has to carry it, or it is reporting an arbitrary cut as a measurement.
Note also that the scores are not calibrated (see above), so the gap
between count_matched and half is partly the miscalibration showing up as
a threshold - fixing the calibration would move these points.

Depth sweep
-----------
Models are fitted on FULL-depth training folds and scored on read-
subsampled copies of their own held-out fold. That is the Task 2
situation: we train on depth >= 20 data and predict on SG-NEx, whose
median depth is 3. Labels come from m6ACE-Seq rather than from the
nanopore reads, so they stay valid when reads are dropped.
